  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a direct relationship between 7-O-Methylmangiferin and HepG2, with the drug acting as a target for multiple molecular entities. The primary pathway involves 7-O-Methylmangiferin targeting MIP, which is then targeted by additional compounds, including CID 21582484 and 5-Hydroxy-6,8-Dimethoxy-2,3-Dimethylbenzo[G]Chromen-4-One. These molecular entities are all implicated in the interaction with HepG2, a human hepatocellular carcinoma cell line. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism where 7-O-Methylmangiferin may influence HepG2 through the activation of these downstream targets, possibly via a common signaling pathway or by modulating the activity of the target molecules. The evidence supports a role for 7-O-Methylmangiferin in interacting with HepG2 through a network of molecular targets, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
